<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<project xmlns="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0"
         xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
         xsi:schemaLocation="http://maven.apache.org/POM/4.0.0 https://maven.apache.org/xsd/maven-4.0.0.xsd">
    <modelVersion>4.0.0</modelVersion>

    <!--
        STANDALONE, AND DELIBERATELY NOT A `jcodebuddy` ARTIFACT.

        This project has its own group id, its own artifact id, its own version and no parent: it does not
        inherit `jcodebuddy-parent`, and nothing about it belongs to the JCodeBuddy namespace. That is a
        decision about what it is, not tidiness — the file-system state contract is not a JCodeBuddy feature.
        It is a small library a watcher and any number of other tools share, it depends on nothing from this
        checkout except the `wyhash` project's Java artifact once that lands, and a tool that wants it should
        be able to take it without taking anything else.

        WHERE IT SITS AND WHY. The directory is at the root of this checkout and the root POM still lists it
        as a `<module>`: the reactor is how it is built and how the recorded gate runs its tests, while the
        *coordinates* are what make it standalone. Being in a reactor is a build relationship, not a
        namespace — the same distinction the `watch/` standalone rule makes about the shared build parent.

        `java-watch*` MAY DEPEND ON THIS PROJECT. The watcher family's rule is that it must not know about
        Jackson, OpenRewrite, or this workspace's `jcodebuddy-*` / `hipster-*` / `metadata-*` / `jwa-*`
        artifacts (AGENTS.md § 2, `scripts/check-watch-standalone.js`). `fs-meta` is none of those: it is a
        standalone package with its own identity, no workspace dependency and no JCodeBuddy vocabulary, so the
        watcher may consume it — which is the point, because the watcher is the writer of the trio. The
        amendment is recorded in `AGENTS.md` and in the check's own header.
    -->

    <groupId>hr.hrg</groupId>
    <artifactId>fs-meta</artifactId>
    <version>0.1.0-SNAPSHOT</version>
    <packaging>jar</packaging>

    <name>fs-meta</name>
    <description>
        The file-system state of one codebase folder as a trio of files — a compact binary index
        (files.meta), its LF-delimited names (files.txt) and a JSON sidekick (files.json) — so that a
        watcher can maintain it once and every other tool can ask "is this file still what I indexed?"
        without walking the tree. The format is specified in FORMAT.md; the Java, Bun and (later) Zig
        implementations all assert the same conformance vectors.
    </description>

    <!--
        No parent means no managed versions, so everything this build uses is pinned here. The values are the
        ones `jcodebuddy-parent` pins, kept identical on purpose: this project is built by the same reactor and
        the same JDK 25, and a different compiler or surefire version here would be a difference nobody asked
        for.
    -->
    <properties>
        <project.build.sourceEncoding>UTF-8</project.build.sourceEncoding>
        <maven.compiler.release>25</maven.compiler.release>

        <wyhash.version>1.1.0-SNAPSHOT</wyhash.version>
        <gitignore.version>0.2.0</gitignore.version>
        <junit.version>5.10.2</junit.version>

        <maven.compiler.plugin.version>3.11.0</maven.compiler.plugin.version>
        <maven.surefire.plugin.version>3.2.5</maven.surefire.plugin.version>
        <maven.jar.plugin.version>3.3.0</maven.jar.plugin.version>
        <maven.install.plugin.version>3.1.4</maven.install.plugin.version>
        <maven.deploy.plugin.version>3.1.2</maven.deploy.plugin.version>
    </properties>

    <dependencies>
        <!--
            The one hash the format uses: `wyhash64`, seed 0 — the same coordinates this file has always
            resolved, now at `1.1.0-SNAPSHOT` because the implementation this project needs (zero-allocation
            `String`, `MemorySegment`, the generated vectors) lives there and `1.0.0` did not have it.

            THE HASH IS NOT BUILT HERE (DEC-050). `hr.hrg:wyhash` has its own repository
            (github.com/hrgdavor/wyhash); this dependency resolves from the local repository, so a machine needs
            it installed once — `cd <wyhash checkout> && mvn -o install -Dgpg.skip=true` — or, once 1.1.0 is
            published, from Maven Central. `mvn -pl :fs-meta` therefore does NOT build its own dependency, and
            `-am` no longer reaches it: a "cannot resolve hr.hrg:wyhash" failure means the install is missing,
            not that the POM is wrong.

            Both ports of this format still hash with the SAME CODE rather than with the same algorithm: this
            artifact is the Java half, and the Bun half imports the same project's `js/wyhash64.mjs` through the
            package link the root `package.json` declares. The vectors prove it either way —
            `conformance/fsmeta-vectors.json`'s `hashCases` pin the values, and the wyhash project's own
            generated vectors pin them against the Zig builtin reference.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>hr.hrg</groupId>
            <artifactId>wyhash</artifactId>
            <version>${wyhash.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <!--
            The `.gitignore` matcher, from the project that owns it: `hr.hrg:gitignore` (DEC-051). This project
            implements no matcher of its own and carries no copy — `FsMetaScanner` calls
            `hr.hrg.gitignore.GitIgnoreFile` / `GitIgnoreRules`, whose API is the one the deleted local copies
            had. The Zig side takes the same project through `build.zig.zon` at the same version, so all three
            walks are the same rules.

            Resolved the way `wyhash` is: from the local repository (install the gitignore checkout once with
            `mvn install`), or from Maven Central once 0.2.0 is released. A "cannot resolve hr.hrg:gitignore"
            failure means that install is missing.
        -->
        <dependency>
            <groupId>hr.hrg</groupId>
            <artifactId>gitignore</artifactId>
            <version>${gitignore.version}</version>
        </dependency>

        <dependency>
            <groupId>org.junit.jupiter</groupId>
            <artifactId>junit-jupiter</artifactId>
            <version>${junit.version}</version>
            <scope>test</scope>
        </dependency>
    </dependencies>

    <build>
        <plugins>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-compiler-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven.compiler.plugin.version}</version>
                <configuration>
                    <release>${maven.compiler.release}</release>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-surefire-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven.surefire.plugin.version}</version>
                <configuration>
                    <parallel>none</parallel>
                </configuration>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-jar-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven.jar.plugin.version}</version>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-install-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven.install.plugin.version}</version>
            </plugin>
            <plugin>
                <groupId>org.apache.maven.plugins</groupId>
                <artifactId>maven-deploy-plugin</artifactId>
                <version>${maven.deploy.plugin.version}</version>
            </plugin>
        </plugins>
    </build>
</project>